 "case:briefing.0": "Croyde, North Devon. Wednesday 22 November 2023. Out of season the village belongs to the surfers and the sheep: the car parks are empty, half the cottages are dark, and the wind comes straight off the Atlantic.",
 "case:briefing.1": "On Friday morning Tess Rendle, 31, an A&E nurse at the hospital in Barnstaple, finished four night shifts in a row, and that evening she borrowed her brother’s camper van, Moss, to sleep by the sea. She parked at the clifftop car park at Marram Point. At 08:40 on Saturday the man who runs the café there found her dead inside, with the diesel heater still running. Carbon monoxide.",
 "case:briefing.2": "The police vehicle examiner found the heater’s exhaust leaking into the van: no gasket in the joint, and the clamp loose. Her brother, Callum, builds camper vans for a living, and he fitted that heater himself. Nobody else is suspected. Callum has been interviewed under caution about his work, the coroner has the file, Trading Standards want his records, and online he is already ‘the van man who gassed his own sister’.",
 "case:briefing.3": "Their mother, Ruth Rendle, has hired you. ‘Tess treated carbon monoxide poisoning every winter. She would never have slept next to a heater she didn’t trust. And Callum never skips a step — never. I’ve lost my daughter. Don’t let me lose my son as well.’",
 "case:briefing.4": "Moss is in the police compound in Barnstaple, waiting for the coroner; Callum’s solicitor has arranged for you to see it. Callum has given you the keys to everything else.",
 "case:epilogue.0": "Pete Stannard said nothing at all for a day. Then he asked for a pen and wrote four pages, and the last line was the same as the first: ‘It should have been him.’",
 "case:epilogue.1": "He had planned it for eighteen months: the unit next door, the mugs of tea, the borrowed ramp, the MOT that let him study Moss’s heater at his leisure. He couldn’t touch it then: Callum checked it every Thursday and would have found it. It had to be after the last check and before the Friday night, so he came in at six, as he always did, before anyone else, and went out of his back door with a torch. Callum’s own heater, Callum’s own work. He wanted it to look like carelessness, he wrote, because carelessness was what had killed his son. His thumbprint was on the sticky side of the tape. The gasket from Moss’s heater was in the drain outside his back door, where he had dropped it when Kai turned up early for his tea. By torchlight, kneeling on wet gravel, he had never noticed the line of yellow paint across the nut.",
 "case:epilogue.2": "He hadn’t known about Tess. He drove out of the yard at 11:58; Callum told the others just after twelve. On Saturday, in a hotel room in Exeter, he heard that a young woman had died in Callum Rendle’s van, and understood that he had made another family like his own. He was charged with her murder.",
 "case:epilogue.3": "The coroner suspended Tess’s inquest the day Pete was charged. Trading Standards closed their file on Saltworks Vans, and the internet moved on without apologising. Callum never reopened Rendle Surf. Moss sits in a police garage in Exeter, waiting for the trial, and Callum has told them he never wants her back.",
 "case:epilogue.4": "Lou Bray paid back every heater and told Kai she was sorry. Jo Tremlett turned off the location sharing at last. Gwen Westcott still leaves notes on vans at Marram Point, but these days they begin ‘Please’. And one cold morning in December, Dan Kellow and Callum Rendle, who hadn’t spoken properly in four years, sat on the bench at the cliff edge and talked until long after the café should have opened.",
 "case:epilogue.5": "By the spring there were two benches at Marram Point, side by side, facing the same waves. KIERAN MOYLE · He loved these waves. TESS RENDLE · 1992–2023 · She needed the sea.",
 "case:hints.h_accuse.levels.0": "Moss was rigged before anyone knew Tess would be in it. Think about who that makes the target — and who was in the yard.",
 "case:hints.h_accuse.levels.1": "Somebody blamed Callum for a death long before Tess died.",
 "case:hints.h_accuse.levels.2": "The killer is Pete Stannard, Kieran Moyle’s father: he loosened the heater’s exhaust joint and taped over the alarm’s sensor, in revenge for a drowning. Prove it with your answer about Friday morning, the gasket, and your answer about Kieran’s father.",
 "case:hints.h_accuse_ready.levels.0": "For each proof slot, ask: which of your cards shows Pete had the chance, which shows what he used, and which shows why?",
 "case:hints.h_accuse_ready.levels.1": "The chance: what Kai saw, your answer about Friday morning, or the timeline. The means: what was found by Pete’s back door or in his bench drawer. The motive: the newspaper cutting, or your answer about Kieran’s father.",
