 "case:locations.cottage.description": "A pebble-dashed cottage at the top of the village, with surfboards racked along the side wall. Inside it smells of wet neoprene and toast. Callum is at the yard; his wife, Abby, has dropped their daughter at nursery and let you in.",
 "case:locations.cottage.hotspots.hs_ct_boards.label": "The boards",
 "case:locations.cottage.hotspots.hs_ct_boards.text": "Six surfboards in a rack, one with a snapped fin, and a changing robe on a hook. Callum’s wetsuit hangs on a hook, still damp: he was in the water off the beach below the house on Saturday morning when Dan rang.",
 "case:locations.cottage.hotspots.hs_ct_calendar.label": "Family calendar",
 "case:locations.cottage.hotspots.hs_ct_calendar.text": "The calendar on the fridge, under a magnet shaped like a wave.",
 "case:locations.cottage.hotspots.hs_ct_desk.label": "Callum’s desk",
 "case:locations.cottage.hotspots.hs_ct_desk.text": "Invoices, a red-topped letter from HMRC, a mug of pens and a ring binder labelled ‘SALTWORKS — INSURANCE’.",
 "case:locations.cottage.hotspots.hs_ct_drawer.label": "Desk drawer",
 "case:locations.cottage.hotspots.hs_ct_drawer.text": "The drawer Callum told you about. Under the chequebooks lies a newspaper cutting, gone soft from being folded and unfolded.",
 "case:locations.cottage.hotspots.hs_ct_garage.label": "The garage",
 "case:locations.cottage.hotspots.hs_ct_garage.text": "Old surf school gear: foam boards, a stack of rash vests, a sun-faded flag. On the workbench, a box fresh from the printer.",
 "case:locations.cottage.hotspots.hs_ct_phone.label": "Callum’s phone",
 "case:locations.cottage.hotspots.hs_ct_phone.text": "Callum’s old phone, on charge on the desk: he’s been using the shop phone since Saturday, because he can’t face this one. Dozens of unread messages. Abby rings him, and he tells her you can look.",
 "case:locations.cottage.hotspots.hs_ct_table.label": "Kitchen table",
 "case:locations.cottage.hotspots.hs_ct_table.text": "Abby Rendle pours you a coffee and sits with her hands round her own. There’s a child’s drawing of a green van on the fridge behind her.",
 "case:locations.cottage.name": "Callum’s Cottage",
 "case:locations.cottage.summary": "Callum and Abby’s home at the top of Croyde.",
 "case:locations.house.art.props.0.label": "Window",
 "case:locations.house.art.props.1.label": "Desk",
 "case:locations.house.art.props.2.label": "Bed",
 "case:locations.house.art.props.3.label": "Clothes rail",
 "case:locations.house.art.props.4.label": "Noticeboard",
 "case:locations.house.art.props.6.label": "Kitchen",
 "case:locations.house.art.props.7.label": "Kitchen table",
 "case:locations.house.art.props.8.label": "Fridge",
 "case:locations.house.description": "A terraced house near the hospital that Tess shared with Megan Hale, another nurse. Tess’s room is at the front: a single bed, a rail of scrubs and a wetsuit, a desk under the window. Megan has been off work since Saturday, and has made you tea.",
 "case:locations.house.hotspots.hs_th_board.label": "Noticeboard",
 "case:locations.house.hotspots.hs_th_board.text": "Her rota — nights Monday to Thursday, ‘OFF!!!’ on Friday in green pen — a photo of Tess and Callum sitting on Moss’s roof with their boards, and a poster from A&E: ‘Headache? Dizzy? Sick? Everyone in the house feeling it? THINK CARBON MONOXIDE.’",
 "case:locations.house.hotspots.hs_th_desk.label": "Tess’s desk",
 "case:locations.house.hotspots.hs_th_desk.text": "Mortgage papers in a plastic wallet, a stethoscope, a tide clock that has stopped, and a notebook with a wave drawn on the cover.",
 "case:locations.house.hotspots.hs_th_fridge.label": "Fridge",
 "case:locations.house.hotspots.hs_th_fridge.text": "A rota for the bins, a magnet from Croyde, and a sticky note in Tess’s round handwriting: ‘Flat!! Completion 12 Jan 🏠’.",
 "case:locations.house.hotspots.hs_th_phone.label": "Megan’s phone",
 "case:locations.house.hotspots.hs_th_phone.text": "Megan opens her messages with Tess and hands you the phone. She screenshotted Tess’s story on Friday night, to tease her about it.",
 "case:locations.house.hotspots.hs_th_rail.label": "Clothes rail",
 "case:locations.house.hotspots.hs_th_rail.text": "Navy scrubs, a winter wetsuit still sandy at the ankles, and a hoodie that says ‘Saltworks Vans — Crew’ across the back.",
 "case:locations.house.hotspots.hs_th_table.label": "Kitchen table",
 "case:locations.house.hotspots.hs_th_table.text": "Megan sits down opposite you with both hands round her mug. She looks as if she hasn’t slept since Saturday.",
 "case:locations.house.name": "Tess’s House",
 "case:locations.house.summary": "The house share in Barnstaple where Tess lived.",
 "case:locations.rearyard.art.props.0.label": "Fence — bramble field beyond",
 "case:locations.rearyard.art.props.1.label": "Moss’s spot",
 "case:locations.rearyard.art.props.2.label": "Recycling cage",
 "case:locations.rearyard.art.props.3.label": "Drain",
 "case:locations.rearyard.art.props.4.label": "Unit 4",
 "case:locations.rearyard.art.props.5.label": "Unit 5 — Stannard Motors",
 "case:locations.rearyard.description": "A strip of gravel behind both units, hemmed in by the palisade fence, with a bramble field beyond. Unit 4’s back door opens on to it; so does Unit 5’s, Pete Stannard’s garage. This is where Moss was parked from Thursday afternoon until Callum brought it round the front on Friday morning, out of sight of the gate camera.",
 "case:locations.rearyard.hotspots.hs_ry_cage.label": "Recycling cage",
 "case:locations.rearyard.hotspots.hs_ry_cage.text": "A wire cage of cardboard against the fence, a few steps from Moss’s spot.",
 "case:locations.rearyard.hotspots.hs_ry_counter.label": "Pete’s parts counter",
 "case:locations.rearyard.hotspots.hs_ry_counter.text": "Through Pete’s back door: invoices on a spike, a kettle, two mugs and a jar of teabags. On the wall, a framed MOT tester’s certificate made out to Peter J. Stannard, and a faded photo of a garage forecourt in Swindon.",
 "case:locations.rearyard.hotspots.hs_ry_drain.label": "The drain",
 "case:locations.rearyard.hotspots.hs_ry_drain.text": "A square drain grate in the gravel just outside Pete’s back door. Callum levers it up with a screwdriver. In the silt trap underneath, among the leaves, something small and round and black.",
 "case:locations.rearyard.hotspots.hs_ry_drawer.label": "Pete’s bench drawer",
 "case:locations.rearyard.hotspots.hs_ry_drawer.text": "The drawer under Pete’s workbench: cable ties, fuses, a box of jubilee clips — and a roll of clear tape with its end cut square.",
 "case:locations.rearyard.hotspots.hs_ry_fence.label": "The fence",
 "case:locations.rearyard.hotspots.hs_ry_fence.text": "Two and a half metres of palisade, each upright split into three spikes, and brambles thick behind it. Nobody has climbed it; there isn’t a mark on the bramble or the paint.",
 "case:locations.rearyard.hotspots.hs_ry_photo.label": "Pete’s office",
 "case:locations.rearyard.hotspots.hs_ry_photo.text": "Off the back of Pete’s workshop, a cupboard of an office that nobody else goes into: a desk, a filing cabinet, a calendar from a tyre company, and on the wall above the desk a photo in a clip frame.",
 "case:locations.rearyard.hotspots.hs_ry_spot.label": "Moss’s spot",
 "case:locations.rearyard.hotspots.hs_ry_spot.text": "A rectangle of gravel, flattened where Moss stood on Thursday night. From here you can’t see the gate at all, and the gate camera can’t see you: the units are in the way.",
 "case:locations.rearyard.hotspots.hs_ry_u4door.label": "Unit 4’s back door",
 "case:locations.rearyard.hotspots.hs_ry_u4door.text": "Saltworks’ back door, steel, with an alarm contact on the frame. Nobody uses it much.",
 "case:locations.rearyard.hotspots.hs_ry_u5door.label": "Unit 5’s back door",
 "case:locations.rearyard.hotspots.hs_ry_u5door.text": "The back door of Unit 5, Pete’s garage. Shut. Somewhere inside, a radio.",
 "case:locations.rearyard.name": "The Rear Yard",
 "case:locations.rearyard.summary": "Behind Units 4 and 5, where Moss stood the night before.",
 "case:locations.workshop.art.props.0.label": "Moss’s empty bay",
 "case:locations.workshop.art.props.1.label": "Workbench",
 "case:locations.workshop.art.props.2.label": "Office",
 "case:locations.workshop.art.props.3.label": "Stockroom",
 "case:locations.workshop.art.props.4.label": "Back door",
 "case:locations.workshop.art.props.5.label": "Roller door",
 "case:locations.workshop.art.props.6.label": "Front yard",
 "case:locations.workshop.art.props.7.label": "Gate & camera",
 "case:locations.workshop.description": "Unit 4 on a small industrial estate outside Braunton: a workshop with a roller door, an office and a stockroom, inside a steel palisade fence with a single gate. Moss’s usual bay is empty. Callum is at the bench, sanding the same cupboard door over and over.",
 "case:locations.workshop.hotspots.hs_ws_bay.label": "Moss’s bay",
 "case:locations.workshop.hotspots.hs_ws_bay.text": "An empty bay with an oil stain the shape of a van. A whiteboard on the wall: the Jenkinses’ kitchen pod, two half-built conversions, and ‘MOSS — cupboard catch’ ticked off in Callum’s writing.",
 "case:locations.workshop.hotspots.hs_ws_bench.label": "Callum’s workbench",
 "case:locations.workshop.hotspots.hs_ws_bench.text": "Callum’s bench: clamps, a heat gun, a tin of yellow paint pens, and a tablet propped against a jar of rivets, open at Saltworks’ build albums. There’s an album for every van they’ve ever built.",
 "case:locations.workshop.hotspots.hs_ws_gate.label": "The gate",
 "case:locations.workshop.hotspots.hs_ws_gate.text": "The only way into the yard: a steel gate in a fence of spiked palisade, two and a half metres high, all the way round both units. Three people have keys to it: Callum, Lou and Pete. A camera on the gatepost watches the gate and the fronts of Units 4 and 5. It can’t see round the back.",
 "case:locations.workshop.hotspots.hs_ws_office.label": "The office",
 "case:locations.workshop.hotspots.hs_ws_office.text": "Callum logs you in to the gate camera’s recordings on the office computer. It keeps thirty days, with a gap most nights: the router updates itself at one in the morning, and the camera doesn’t always come back. Lou has been on at the internet company about it for months.",
 "case:locations.workshop.hotspots.hs_ws_panel.label": "Alarm panel",
 "case:locations.workshop.hotspots.hs_ws_panel.text": "Unit 4’s alarm panel, by the office door. Callum scrolls back to Thursday night: who set and unset it, and which sensors fired.",
 "case:locations.workshop.hotspots.hs_ws_stock.label": "The stockroom",
 "case:locations.workshop.hotspots.hs_ws_stock.text": "Shelves of boxed heaters, fridges and leisure batteries, with a stock sheet on a clipboard. While you count, Callum takes out his phone and starts searching.",
 "case:locations.workshop.name": "Saltworks Vans",
 "case:locations.workshop.summary": "Callum’s workshop in Braunton.",
 "case:setting": "Croyde · North Devon · November 2023",
 "case:sketchCredit": "SKETCH: SITE VISIT, 22 NOVEMBER 2023 · NOT TO SCALE",
 "case:sketchTitle": "CROYDE",
 "case:stayLabel": "Stay in Croyde",
 "case:tagline": "A borrowed camper van, a cold night by the sea, and a heater that should never have failed.",
 "case:timeline.conclusion": "Moss was rigged at dawn on Friday, in the rear yard, while only Pete was there. At 11:40 Tess asked for the van; at 11:58 Pete left for Exeter; just after twelve Callum told the others. When Pete drove away, he believed Callum would be the one sleeping beside that heater.",
 "case:timeline.events.tl_ask.text": "Tess asks to borrow Moss",
 "case:timeline.events.tl_ask.time": "Fri 11:40",
 "case:timeline.events.tl_check.teaser": "Something done at the yard on Thursday.",
 "case:timeline.events.tl_check.text": "Callum’s weekly heater check: the alarm reads zero",
 "case:timeline.events.tl_check.time": "Thu 17:41",
 "case:timeline.events.tl_collect.teaser": "Something at the yard on Friday afternoon.",
 "case:timeline.events.tl_collect.text": "Callum tests the alarm, and Tess drives Moss away",
 "case:timeline.events.tl_collect.time": "Fri 15:44",
 "case:timeline.events.tl_found.text": "Dan finds Tess",
 "case:timeline.events.tl_found.time": "Sat 08:40",
 "case:timeline.events.tl_jo.teaser": "A visitor.",
 "case:timeline.events.tl_jo.text": "Jo argues with Tess in the van",
 "case:timeline.events.tl_jo.time": "Fri 21:03",
 "case:timeline.events.tl_kai.teaser": "Something someone noticed.",
 "case:timeline.events.tl_kai.text": "Kai sees Pete come in from the rear yard",
 "case:timeline.events.tl_kai.time": "Fri 08:00",
 "case:timeline.events.tl_lou.teaser": "Something the gate camera saw.",
 "case:timeline.events.tl_lou.text": "Lou carries boxes out of the yard",
 "case:timeline.events.tl_lou.time": "Thu 22:40",
 "case:timeline.events.tl_note.teaser": "Another visitor.",
 "case:timeline.events.tl_note.text": "Gwen leaves her note on Moss",
 "case:timeline.events.tl_note.time": "Fri 23:09",
 "case:timeline.events.tl_pete.teaser": "Something else the gate camera saw.",
 "case:timeline.events.tl_pete.text": "Pete leaves for Exeter",
 "case:timeline.events.tl_pete.time": "Fri 11:58",
 "case:timeline.events.tl_rigged.teaser": "Something done to Moss.",
 "case:timeline.events.tl_rigged.text": "Moss is rigged",
 "case:timeline.events.tl_story.teaser": "Something Tess posted.",
 "case:timeline.events.tl_story.text": "Tess posts a story: heater on, kettle on",
 "case:timeline.events.tl_story.time": "Fri 19:31",
 "case:timeline.events.tl_told.teaser": "Something said at the yard.",
 "case:timeline.events.tl_told.text": "Callum tells Lou and Kai that Tess is taking Moss",
 "case:timeline.prompt": "Put the events in the order they really happened. Times you know for certain are shown; reason out the rest.",
 "case:timeline.title": "Thursday to Saturday, reconstructed",
 "case:title": "Undertow",
 "deductions:contradictions.c_callum_row.insight": "Callum owed Tess the £18,000 deposit for the flat she was buying, and couldn’t pay it back. They rowed about it at Sunday lunch, five days before she died.",
 "deductions:contradictions.c_callum_row.response.0": "…She lent me the money in February, when Saltworks nearly went under. Her whole deposit. She completes in January. I promised her Christmas.",
 "deductions:contradictions.c_callum_row.response.1": "On Sunday at Mum’s I told her I couldn’t. Not till the spring. She said I was always going to be like this, and she was right. The last proper conversation we had was a row.",
 "deductions:contradictions.c_callum_row.response.2": "And on Friday she texted me as if nothing had happened, and asked for Moss. That was Tess.",
 "deductions:contradictions.c_callum_row.title": "Tess and Callum had fallen out",
 "deductions:contradictions.c_dan_contact.insight": "Two days before Tess died, Dan sent Callum a threat, and on Thursday evening he turned up at Saltworks’ gate to shout at him. He has never forgiven Callum for blaming him at Kieran’s inquest.",
 "deductions:contradictions.c_dan_contact.response.0": "…All right. I sent him a message on Wednesday night. And I went round there on Thursday.",
 "deductions:contradictions.c_dan_contact.response.1": "He’s starting the school again. After everything. He stood up at that inquest and said it was my positioning — and it was, partly, I’ve had four years to know that — but he’s the one who left me alone with nine kids in a rip.",
 "deductions:contradictions.c_dan_contact.response.2": "I shouted. He turned his back on me and went round the side to get his phone, and I followed him, still shouting. Then he walked me back to my car. I never laid a finger on his van.",
 "deductions:contradictions.c_dan_contact.title": "Dan threatened Callum",
 "deductions:contradictions.c_gwen_never.insight": "The quad bike and head torch at 23:09 were Gwen’s, and the note on Moss is in the same block capitals as the sign on her gate. She lied because she has let down vanlifers’ tyres before, and was afraid of what else the police might ask.",
 "deductions:contradictions.c_gwen_never.response.0": "…All right. Yes. I go round most nights with a torch, and I left that note.",
 "deductions:contradictions.c_gwen_never.response.1": "I didn’t tell the police because they’d have asked about the tyres. I’ve let a few down over the years. Not that one. Not that night.",
 "deductions:contradictions.c_gwen_never.response.2": "The windows were running wet inside, and the heater was ticking away underneath — you can hear them, those little diesel things. I thought, well, at least she’s warm. I put my note under the wiper and went home.",
 "deductions:contradictions.c_gwen_never.title": "Gwen left the note",
 "deductions:contradictions.c_jo_findmy.insight": "Tess’s story had no location on it. Jo and Tess still shared their locations from when they lived together, and Jo still watched. On Friday night she saw Tess’s dot stop at Marram Point.",
 "deductions:contradictions.c_jo_findmy.response.0": "…We never turned it off. Location sharing. From when we lived together.",
 "deductions:contradictions.c_jo_findmy.response.1": "I’d look at it at work. At night. I told myself it was just so I’d know she was safe. On Friday I watched her dot go out to Marram Point and stop, and I got in the car.",
 "deductions:contradictions.c_jo_findmy.response.2": "I know what that makes me. It doesn’t make me a killer.",
 "deductions:contradictions.c_jo_findmy.title": "Jo was watching her location",
 "deductions:contradictions.c_jo_home.insight": "Jo drove out to the car park at nine with a dozen roses and spent forty minutes in the van with Tess. She lied because she was the last person to see Tess alive — and she left her there.",
 "deductions:contradictions.c_jo_home.response.0": "…Okay. Okay. I went.",
 "deductions:contradictions.c_jo_home.response.1": "I took her roses. I wanted to say sorry properly, to her face. We argued, she told me to go, and I went. At twenty to ten she was alive and furious with me. That’s the last time I saw her.",
 "deductions:contradictions.c_jo_home.response.2": "I didn’t say because I knew how it would look. The ex who turns up in the dark.",
 "deductions:contradictions.c_jo_home.title": "Jo was at Marram Point",
 "deductions:contradictions.c_lou_home.insight": "At 22:40 on Thursday, Lou let herself into the yard and spent a quarter of an hour there — on the night Moss stood unwatched behind Unit 4.",
 "deductions:contradictions.c_lou_home.response.0": "…I popped in. For the VAT paperwork. The return’s due and I can’t concentrate in the day with the radio going.",
 "deductions:contradictions.c_lou_home.response.1": "Fifteen minutes. I was in and out.",
 "deductions:contradictions.c_lou_home.title": "Lou was in the yard on Thursday night",
 "deductions:contradictions.c_lou_paperwork.insight": "The camera shows Lou carrying six boxes to her car. Six heaters are missing from the stockroom, and six are for sale online, collection Braunton. Lou has been selling Saltworks’ stock since August — and letting Callum blame Kai.",
 "deductions:contradictions.c_lou_paperwork.response.0": "…Since August. Since Steve lost his job at the car dealership and our mortgage went from seven hundred a month to thirteen hundred.",
 "deductions:contradictions.c_lou_paperwork.response.1": "I sell a few on the side. I always meant to pay it back. And Callum blamed Kai, and I let him.",
 "deductions:contradictions.c_lou_paperwork.response.2": "But I went in the front, I went to the stockroom, and I went out the front. I never went out the back. I never touched Moss.",
 "deductions:contradictions.c_lou_paperwork.title": "Lou was stealing heaters",
 "deductions:contradictions.c_lou_rear.insight": "Unit 4’s alarm shows the rear door open for forty-two seconds at 22:49 — the door on to the rear yard, where Moss stood. Lou said she never went out the back.",
 "deductions:contradictions.c_lou_rear.response.0": "…The cartons. Oh God, I forgot the cartons.",
 "deductions:contradictions.c_lou_rear.response.1": "Six heaters wouldn’t fit in my car in their big outer boxes, so I took them out, flattened the cartons and shoved them in the recycling cage out the back. Thirty seconds. I didn’t look at Moss. I didn’t even put the light on.",
 "deductions:contradictions.c_lou_rear.title": "Lou went out of the back door",
 "deductions:contradictions.c_pete_coolant.insight": "The vehicle examiner found nothing wrong with Moss’s engine or cooling system. And what sits under Moss’s passenger seat isn’t the engine. It’s the heater.",
 "deductions:contradictions.c_pete_coolant.response.0": "…I don’t know what you want me to say.",
 "deductions:contradictions.c_pete_coolant.response.1": "I smelled something, I looked, I put it back. That’s all. That’s not a crime.",
 "deductions:contradictions.c_pete_coolant.title": "There was no coolant leak",
 "deductions:contradictions.c_pete_moss.insight": "Just before eight on Friday, Kai saw Pete come in through his back door from the rear yard, with a torch and wet knees. Pete told him he’d had Moss’s seat base off — the seat over the heater.",
 "deductions:contradictions.c_pete_moss.response.0": "…All right. I was in early. I always am — I don’t sleep.",
 "deductions:contradictions.c_pete_moss.response.1": "I thought I smelled coolant when I came in, round the back. Moss was nearest, so I had a look. Seat base off, torch, nothing there. Put it back. That’s all it was.",
 "deductions:contradictions.c_pete_moss.title": "Pete was at Moss on Friday morning",
 "deductions:contradictions.c_pete_nephew.insight": "The boy on Pete’s office wall is no nephew. Kieran Moyle had his mother’s surname; his father was a mechanic from Swindon who brought him to Croyde every August — and who told the inquest that somebody should answer for his death.",
 "deductions:contradictions.c_pete_nephew.response.0": "Pete takes off his glasses and rubs his eyes. When he speaks again his voice is very quiet.",
 "deductions:contradictions.c_pete_nephew.response.1": "Kieran. His mum gave him her name. I had him summers.",
 "deductions:contradictions.c_pete_nephew.response.2": "We came down every August, him and me. I had a son, and he drowned three hundred yards from where Callum Rendle was teaching some rich kid to stand up on a board. I came down here because I wanted to live by the sea he loved. That’s all.",
 "deductions:contradictions.c_pete_nephew.title": "Kieran was Pete’s son",
 "deductions:contradictions.c_pete_newcomer.insight": "The boy in the photo in Pete’s office is on Croyde beach in August 2019, at his first surf lesson, and Pete keeps him on the wall above his desk. Pete knew Croyde long before he took the unit next to Callum’s.",
 "deductions:contradictions.c_pete_newcomer.response.0": "Pete looks at the photo for a moment too long.",
 "deductions:contradictions.c_pete_newcomer.response.1": "That’s my sister’s lad. On his holidays — she sent me that. I’ve never been down there with him. I’d not been to Devon in my life till I came for the unit.",
 "deductions:contradictions.c_pete_newcomer.response.2": "Nice-looking boy, isn’t he.",
 "deductions:contradictions.c_pete_newcomer.title": "Pete knew Croyde long before Unit 5",
 "deductions:contradictions.c_pete_rushed.insight": "To Kai, Pete always called Callum’s installs the tidiest on the estate. He told the police the opposite on Monday, two days after Tess died in one of them.",
 "deductions:contradictions.c_pete_rushed.response.0": "…Kai’s a kid. He hears what he wants to hear.",
 "deductions:contradictions.c_pete_rushed.response.1": "Neat isn’t the same as safe. That’s all I meant.",
 "deductions:contradictions.c_pete_rushed.title": "Pete never thought Callum’s work was rushed",
 "deductions:deductions.d_alarm.conclusion": "A strip of clear tape covers the grille where the gas reaches the sensor. The TEST button only checks the electronics and the sounder, so the alarm still shrieks whenever anyone tests it — it passed for the police — but it could never smell the gas.",
 "deductions:deductions.d_alarm.options.0": "Its batteries had run flat",
 "deductions:deductions.d_alarm.options.1": "It was mounted too far from the heater",
 "deductions:deductions.d_alarm.options.2": "Tess switched it off when it chirped",
 "deductions:deductions.d_alarm.options.3": "Someone had taped over its sensor",
 "deductions:deductions.d_alarm.proofLabels.0": "What’s wrong with the alarm",
 "deductions:deductions.d_alarm.proofLabels.1": "Why nobody noticed",
 "deductions:deductions.d_alarm.question": "Why didn’t Moss’s carbon monoxide alarm wake Tess?",
 "deductions:deductions.d_alarm.title": "A blinded alarm",
 "deductions:deductions.d_dan.conclusion": "Dan was out of the camera’s sight for three minutes on Thursday evening — at Moss, but with Callum, who had gone round for his phone and never let him out of arm’s reach. Undoing the heater joint takes ten minutes at least. He has no key to the gate, the fence is two and a half metres of spiked steel, and early on Friday he was at sea. He never had the chance.",
 "deductions:deductions.d_dan.options.0": "Yes — in the three minutes he was out of the camera’s sight",
 "deductions:deductions.d_dan.options.1": "No — Callum never let him out of arm’s reach",
 "deductions:deductions.d_dan.options.2": "Yes — he climbed back over the fence after dark",
 "deductions:deductions.d_dan.options.3": "No — he was at sea with the lifeboat",
 "deductions:deductions.d_dan.proofLabels.0": "What the camera saw",
 "deductions:deductions.d_dan.proofLabels.1": "What happened out of shot",
 "deductions:deductions.d_dan.question": "Could Dan have rigged Moss on Thursday evening?",
 "deductions:deductions.d_dan.title": "Never out of reach",
 "deductions:deductions.d_father.conclusion": "Kieran Moyle, who drowned on Callum’s lesson in 2019, had his mother’s surname. The cutting says his father was a mechanic from Swindon who brought him to Croyde every August, and told the inquest that somebody should answer for it. Pete is a mechanic who sold up in Swindon, and on his office wall hangs ‘K’ at his first surf lesson, at Croyde, in August 2019. A year and a half after the inquest he took the unit next to Callum’s — and made him tea, and lent him his ramp, and watched him.",
 "deductions:deductions.d_father.options.0": "A Swindon mechanic who never came back to Devon",
 "deductions:deductions.d_father.options.1": "Pete Stannard, the mechanic next door",
 "deductions:deductions.d_father.options.2": "Steve Bray, Lou’s husband",
 "deductions:deductions.d_father.options.3": "Nobody in this case — the family stayed away",
 "deductions:deductions.d_father.proofLabels.0": "What the newspaper says about his father",
 "deductions:deductions.d_father.proofLabels.1": "Who in this case is tied to Kieran",
 "deductions:deductions.d_father.question": "Who was Kieran Moyle’s father?",
 "deductions:deductions.d_father.title": "Kieran’s father",
 "deductions:deductions.d_fitted.conclusion": "Moss’s heater was fitted properly and was working. Since then, somebody has taken the gasket out and loosened the clamp: the broken paint mark and the bright spanner marks on the nut show it has been turned since it was tightened. Somebody undid Callum’s work.",
 "deductions:deductions.d_fitted.options.0": "Yes — the gasket was never put in",
 "deductions:deductions.d_fitted.options.1": "Yes — the exhaust pipe was the wrong size",
 "deductions:deductions.d_fitted.options.2": "No — it was fitted properly, then undone",
 "deductions:deductions.d_fitted.options.3": "No — the heater itself was faulty from new",
 "deductions:deductions.d_fitted.proofLabels.0": "Signs of tampering",
 "deductions:deductions.d_fitted.proofLabels.1": "It was fitted properly",
 "deductions:deductions.d_fitted.question": "Was Moss’s heater badly fitted, as the police think?",
 "deductions:deductions.d_fitted.title": "Fitted properly, then undone",
 "deductions:deductions.d_friday.conclusion": "At about eight on Friday, Kai saw Pete come in from the rear yard with a torch and wet knees, saying he’d had Moss’s seat base off — the seat over the heater. The examiner found nothing wrong with Moss’s cooling system. What Pete was doing under that seat turned up in the drain outside his back door: the gasket from Moss’s heater.",
 "deductions:deductions.d_friday.options.0": "Checking a coolant leak, as he told Kai",
 "deductions:deductions.d_friday.options.1": "Fetching a tool Callum had borrowed",
 "deductions:deductions.d_friday.options.2": "Nothing — Kai has got his days mixed up",
 "deductions:deductions.d_friday.options.3": "Loosening the heater’s exhaust and taping over the alarm",
 "deductions:deductions.d_friday.proofLabels.0": "He was at Moss",
 "deductions:deductions.d_friday.proofLabels.1": "It wasn’t coolant",
 "deductions:deductions.d_friday.question": "What was Pete doing at Moss early on Friday?",
 "deductions:deductions.d_friday.title": "Not coolant",
 "deductions:deductions.d_gas.conclusion": "Tess switched the heater on at about half past seven. Within two hours there were headaches in that van — the first sign of carbon monoxide, put down to a row. The leak was there from the moment the heater ran. Nobody who came to Marram Point that night caused it.",
 "deductions:deductions.d_gas.options.0": "On the drive over from the yard",
 "deductions:deductions.d_gas.options.1": "From the moment Tess turned the heater on",
 "deductions:deductions.d_gas.options.2": "Not until after midnight, while Tess slept",
 "deductions:deductions.d_gas.options.3": "Only once someone had tampered with the van that night",
 "deductions:deductions.d_gas.proofLabels.0": "When the heater went on",
 "deductions:deductions.d_gas.proofLabels.1": "The first symptoms",
 "deductions:deductions.d_gas.question": "When did Moss start filling with carbon monoxide?",
 "deductions:deductions.d_gas.title": "From the first minute",
 "deductions:deductions.d_lou.conclusion": "Lou was out of Unit 4’s back door for forty-two seconds at 22:49 — long enough to push six flattened cartons into the recycling cage, nowhere near long enough to take Moss’s seat base off, undo the heater joint, put it all back and tape the alarm. And after 23:14 her car never left her drive, six miles away, until 08:05. She was stealing heaters, not rigging a van.",
 "deductions:deductions.d_lou.options.0": "Yes — she went out of the back door to Moss",
 "deductions:deductions.d_lou.options.1": "Yes — she came back while the camera was down",
 "deductions:deductions.d_lou.options.2": "No — under a minute out the back, and home all night after",
 "deductions:deductions.d_lou.options.3": "No — she never left the stockroom",
 "deductions:deductions.d_lou.proofLabels.0": "Thursday night at the yard",
 "deductions:deductions.d_lou.proofLabels.1": "The rest of the night",
 "deductions:deductions.d_lou.question": "Could Lou have rigged Moss on Thursday night?",
 "deductions:deductions.d_lou.title": "Forty-two seconds",
 "deductions:deductions.d_target.conclusion": "Moss was rigged before half past eight on Friday. Tess only decided she needed the sea when she woke up after her last night shift, and asked at 11:40. Whoever rigged Moss wasn’t waiting for Tess. They were waiting for the man who slept in it at Marram Point every Friday night: Callum.",
 "deductions:deductions.d_target.options.0": "Tess — someone knew she would be in it",
 "deductions:deductions.d_target.options.1": "Anyone — it was meant to look like an accident",
 "deductions:deductions.d_target.options.2": "Callum — Fridays were his night in Moss",
 "deductions:deductions.d_target.options.3": "Callum’s family — Abby and Ruby used to go too",
 "deductions:deductions.d_target.proofLabels.0": "When it was rigged",
 "deductions:deductions.d_target.proofLabels.1": "When Tess decided",
 "deductions:deductions.d_target.question": "Who was Moss rigged to kill?",
 "deductions:deductions.d_target.title": "Meant for Callum",
 "deductions:deductions.d_when.conclusion": "At 17:41 on Thursday the heater ran clean and the alarm’s grille was clear. From half past eight on Friday, Moss stood in front of the open workshop, in sight of everyone, until Tess drove it away. Somebody rigged it in the yard on Thursday night or early on Friday — before Tess had it, and before anyone came near it at Marram Point.",
 "deductions:deductions.d_when.options.0": "At Marram Point, while Tess slept",
 "deductions:deductions.d_when.options.1": "In the yard, on Friday afternoon",
 "deductions:deductions.d_when.options.2": "At its MOT, two weeks earlier",
 "deductions:deductions.d_when.options.3": "In the yard, between Thursday evening and half past eight on Friday",
 "deductions:deductions.d_when.proofLabels.0": "Still fine on Thursday",
 "deductions:deductions.d_when.proofLabels.1": "In plain sight from half past eight",
 "deductions:deductions.d_when.question": "When was Moss tampered with?",
 "deductions:deductions.d_when.title": "Rigged in the yard",
 "dialogues:statements.st_callum_check.text": "On Thursday at about half five I ran Moss’s heater for twenty minutes. The alarm read zero.",
 "dialogues:statements.st_callum_dan.text": "When Dan came on Thursday he followed me round to Moss and back, shouting, never out of arm’s reach. He didn’t touch her.",
 "dialogues:statements.st_callum_debt.text": "I owed Tess £18,000 for her flat deposit and couldn’t pay it back. We rowed about it on Sunday.",
 "dialogues:statements.st_callum_fine.text": "Tess and I never fell out. Not once, not ever.",
 "dialogues:statements.st_callum_friday.text": "From half eight on Friday, Moss sat out front of the workshop all day.",
 "dialogues:statements.st_callum_gasket.text": "I never skip the gasket, and every nut I tighten gets a line of yellow paint across it.",
 "dialogues:statements.st_callum_kieran.text": "In 2019 a boy called Kieran Moyle drowned on one of my lessons. I never met his family.",
 "dialogues:statements.st_callum_minutes.text": "Undoing the heater joint and putting Moss’s seat back takes ten minutes at least, even if you know how.",
 "dialogues:statements.st_callum_routine.text": "Every Friday from October to March I sleep in Moss at Marram Point and surf at first light.",
 "dialogues:statements.st_callum_test.text": "On Friday afternoon I pressed the test button on Moss’s alarm for Tess, and it went off.",
 "dialogues:statements.st_dan_found.text": "On Saturday at 08:40 I found Tess in the van with the heater still running, and got her out.",
 "dialogues:statements.st_dan_nothing.text": "Until I rang him on Saturday, I’d had nothing to do with Callum since the inquest.",
 "dialogues:statements.st_dan_shout.text": "Early on Friday I was at sea with the lifeboat, from twenty to six until twenty past eight.",
 "dialogues:statements.st_dan_yard.text": "On Thursday evening I went to the yard and shouted at Callum. I followed him round the side and back, and he walked me to my car.",
 "dialogues:statements.st_gwen_heater.text": "At ten past eleven Moss’s windows were running wet inside, and the heater was ticking away.",
 "dialogues:statements.st_gwen_never.text": "I wasn’t up at the car park on Friday night.",
 "dialogues:statements.st_jo_findmy.text": "We still shared our locations. I watched her dot stop at Marram Point and drove out.",
 "dialogues:statements.st_jo_headache.text": "The heater had been on since before I got there. By the time I left, we both had headaches.",
 "dialogues:statements.st_jo_home.text": "I was at home on Friday night. All night.",
 "dialogues:statements.st_jo_shift.text": "On Thursday night I was on a night shift on the ambulance, seven till seven.",
 "dialogues:statements.st_jo_story.text": "Tess’s story showed where she was. That’s how I knew.",
 "dialogues:statements.st_jo_visit.text": "I went to the van at nine on Friday with flowers. We argued, and I left at twenty to ten.",
 "dialogues:statements.st_lou_cartons.text": "On Thursday night I put six empty heater cartons in the recycling cage out the back. I was out there less than a minute.",
 "dialogues:statements.st_lou_friday.text": "From half eight on Friday, Moss sat in front of the workshop door all day. Nobody went near it.",
 "dialogues:statements.st_lou_heaters.text": "On Thursday night I took six heaters from the stockroom to sell. I never went out the back.",
 "dialogues:statements.st_lou_home.text": "I was at home all Thursday night.",
 "dialogues:statements.st_lou_paperwork.text": "I only went into the yard on Thursday night for the VAT paperwork.",
 "dialogues:statements.st_lou_told.text": "Just after twelve on Friday, Callum told me and Kai that Tess was having Moss. Pete had already gone.",
 "dialogues:statements.st_pete_coolant.text": "On Friday morning I thought I could smell coolant, so I had Moss’s seat base off for a look. That’s all.",
 "dialogues:statements.st_pete_exeter.text": "I left for Exeter at twelve on Friday and came back on Sunday night.",
 "dialogues:statements.st_pete_friday.text": "On Friday I opened up, did my paperwork and left at twelve. I never went near Moss.",
 "dialogues:statements.st_pete_kieran.text": "Kieran Moyle was my son. Nobody ever answered for his death.",
 "dialogues:statements.st_pete_nephew.text": "The boy in the photo is my sister’s lad. I’d never been down there with him.",
 "dialogues:statements.st_pete_newcomer.text": "I’d never set foot in North Devon before. I saw the unit on a website and sold up in Swindon.",
 "dialogues:statements.st_pete_routine.text": "Every Friday, Callum takes Moss to Marram Point for the dawn patrol. Regular as clockwork.",
 "dialogues:statements.st_pete_rushed.text": "Callum’s been under pressure, and his work’s been rushed. I’d told him so.",
 "dialogues:topics.t_callum_check.lines.0": "Thursday, about half five. I check Moss every Thursday before my Friday night — run the heater for twenty minutes, sit in it, watch the alarm. I even put it on my story, like an idiot.",
 "dialogues:topics.t_callum_check.lines.1": "He finds it on the workshop tablet and hands it to you.",
 "dialogues:topics.t_callum_check.lines.2": "Zero. It read zero. It was fine.",
 "dialogues:topics.t_callum_check.prompt": "When did you last check the heater?",
 "dialogues:topics.t_callum_dan.lines.0": "He was at the gate when I came out — shouting about Kieran, the inquest, the school. I’d left my phone on Moss’s dashboard after the heater check, so I turned my back on him and went round to get it, and he followed me, right on my shoulder, still shouting.",
 "dialogues:topics.t_callum_dan.lines.1": "I got the phone, turned round and walked him all the way back to his car. He was never out of arm’s reach. He didn’t touch Moss. He didn’t even look at her.",
 "dialogues:topics.t_callum_dan.prompt": "What happened when Dan came to the yard?",
 "dialogues:topics.t_callum_enemies.lines.0": "Callum, whoever did this rigged Moss before Tess had even asked for it. They were expecting you.",
 "dialogues:topics.t_callum_enemies.lines.1": "He sits down on an upturned crate as if his legs have gone.",
 "dialogues:topics.t_callum_enemies.lines.2": "Me. It was meant for me. Oh God. Oh God, Tess.",
 "dialogues:topics.t_callum_enemies.lines.3": "I don’t — Dan. Dan Kellow. He sent me this on Wednesday, after I announced the surf school. And on Thursday evening he turned up at the yard, shouting.",
 "dialogues:topics.t_callum_enemies.lines.4": "He pulls the message up on the workshop tablet.",
 "dialogues:topics.t_callum_enemies.prompt": "Who would want you dead?",
 "dialogues:topics.t_callum_friday.lines.0": "Tess texted me at twenty to twelve, out of nowhere: she’d finished her nights and she needed the sea. I said yes. Of course I said yes.",
 "dialogues:topics.t_callum_friday.lines.1": "Moss was out front of the workshop all day — I’d brought her round at half eight to sort out a cupboard catch that kept rattling. Megan dropped Tess off at half three, and I showed her everything: the heater controls, the timer. I pressed the test button on the alarm and it went off. She laughed at me for fussing.",
 "dialogues:topics.t_callum_friday.prompt": "Tell me about Friday.",
 "dialogues:topics.t_callum_heater.lines.0": "Callum, tell me about the heater in Moss.",
 "dialogues:topics.t_callum_heater.lines.1": "I fitted it two years ago — Moss was the first van Saltworks ever did. A two-kilowatt diesel heater under the passenger seat, exhaust straight down through the floor. I’ve fitted fifty-odd of them. I photograph every step — it’s all in the albums on my bench.",
 "dialogues:topics.t_callum_heater.lines.2": "I never skip the gasket. Never. And every nut I tighten gets a line of yellow paint across it, so I can see if it’s ever moved. The police can say what they like.",
 "dialogues:topics.t_callum_heater.prompt": "Tell me about Moss’s heater.",
 "dialogues:topics.t_callum_kieran.lines.0": "I ran a surf school in Croyde for five years. In August 2019 a boy drowned on one of my lessons. Kieran Moyle. Sixteen. He got caught in a rip.",
 "dialogues:topics.t_callum_kieran.lines.1": "I’d left Dan with nine beginners while I gave a private lesson up the beach. Nine. At the inquest I said Dan was badly positioned. He was. But I was the one who left him on his own.",
 "dialogues:topics.t_callum_kieran.lines.2": "I closed the school. I never met the family — the inquest was nearly all on video, because of the pandemic. I kept the newspaper cutting. It’s in my desk drawer at home. I read it more than I should.",
 "dialogues:topics.t_callum_kieran.prompt": "Tell me about Rendle Surf.",
 "dialogues:topics.t_callum_lou.lines.0": "Lou’s my partner, fifty-fifty since we started. She does the carpentry and the books; I do the gas and the electrics. Without her there’d be no business.",
 "dialogues:topics.t_callum_lou.lines.1": "It’s been a horrible year. Heaters keep going missing from the stockroom and I’ve been on at Kai about it. That’s the state I’m in — accusing a nineteen-year-old.",
 "dialogues:topics.t_callum_lou.prompt": "Tell me about Lou.",
 "dialogues:topics.t_callum_minutes.lines.0": "Seat base off — four screws. Loosen the clamp, slide the gasket out, nip the clamp back up so it looks right, seat base back on. Ten minutes, if you knew exactly what you were doing and had the right spanner in your hand. Longer in the dark.",
 "dialogues:topics.t_callum_minutes.lines.1": "The tape on the alarm, a minute. But you’d need both.",
 "dialogues:topics.t_callum_minutes.prompt": "How long would it take to undo the joint?",
 "dialogues:topics.t_callum_pete.lines.0": "Pete? Best neighbour I’ve ever had. Came down last year and took Unit 5. Lends me his ramp, makes Kai tea, never takes a penny for any of it.",
 "dialogues:topics.t_callum_pete.lines.1": "Somebody at the units told the police my work’s been rushed. Maybe it has. Maybe this is all my fault and I just can’t see it.",
 "dialogues:topics.t_callum_pete.prompt": "Tell me about Pete next door.",
 "dialogues:topics.t_callum_routine.lines.0": "Every Friday, October to March. I sleep in her at Marram Point, surf at first light, and I’m at the yard by ten. Dawn patrol. Everyone knows — I’m a bore about it.",
 "dialogues:topics.t_callum_routine.lines.1": "This Friday I gave her to Tess, and I paddled out off the beach below the house on Saturday instead. I was in the water when Dan rang.",
 "dialogues:topics.t_callum_routine.prompt": "Do you use Moss yourself?",
 "dialogues:topics.t_callum_tess.lines.0": "She was my little sister. When she was small she used to sit on Dad’s van roof with me and count the waves in a set.",
 "dialogues:topics.t_callum_tess.lines.1": "We never fell out. Not once, not ever.",
 "dialogues:topics.t_callum_tess.prompt": "Were you and Tess close?",
 "dialogues:topics.t_dan_bench.lines.0": "Me. On the twenty-first of every month.",
 "dialogues:topics.t_dan_bench.lines.1": "I’m not talking about that. If you want to know, ask Callum Rendle. Ask him what he said.",
 "dialogues:topics.t_dan_bench.prompt": "Who leaves the flowers on the bench?",
 "dialogues:topics.t_dan_callum.lines.0": "I taught for his surf school for four summers. I was twenty when I started. Then it closed.",
 "dialogues:topics.t_dan_callum.lines.1": "Until I had to ring him on Saturday, I’d had nothing to do with Callum since the inquest. Nothing.",
 "dialogues:topics.t_dan_callum.prompt": "How do you know Callum?",
 "dialogues:topics.t_dan_camera.lines.0": "Over the door, for break-ins. It sees the end bays. I’ve been through Friday night already — wrote it all down for the police. They didn’t want it.",
 "dialogues:topics.t_dan_camera.lines.1": "He hands you a printed page and a memory stick.",
 "dialogues:topics.t_dan_camera.prompt": "Does the café have a camera?",
 "dialogues:topics.t_dan_found.lines.0": "I came up at half eight to open. The van was in the end bay with its windows running wet and the heater still going, and one of Gwen’s notes on the windscreen.",
 "dialogues:topics.t_dan_found.lines.1": "I knocked. Nothing. The side door wasn’t locked. I got her out on to the gravel and did CPR, with the call handler on speaker, till the ambulance came — ten minutes. It stank of diesel fumes in there, and I was dizzy myself after a minute. She’d been gone for hours; I knew it the moment I touched her.",
 "dialogues:topics.t_dan_found.prompt": "Tell me what you found on Saturday.",
 "dialogues:topics.t_dan_kieran.lines.0": "Sixteen. Funny. Couldn’t stand up on a board to save his life, kept trying anyway. His dad had paid for the whole week.",
 "dialogues:topics.t_dan_kieran.lines.1": "The rip took him and another lad out together. I got the other one. By the time I went back for Kieran I couldn’t see him. I’ve crewed the lifeboat ever since. Make of that what you like.",
 "dialogues:topics.t_dan_kieran.prompt": "Tell me about Kieran.",
 "dialogues:topics.t_dan_shout.lines.0": "At sea. I live in Appledore, two minutes from the lifeboat station, and I crew the inshore boat. My pager went at twenty to six — a fishing boat aground in the estuary. We were back on the slipway at twenty past eight. It’s all logged.",
 "dialogues:topics.t_dan_shout.prompt": "Where were you early on Friday?",
 "dialogues:topics.t_gwen_callum.lines.0": "The van man. Half the vans that park up there have his sticker on the back. I wrote to him once and asked him to tell his customers to sleep somewhere else. He wrote back very politely, and nothing changed.",
 "dialogues:topics.t_gwen_callum.prompt": "Do you know Callum Rendle?",
 "dialogues:topics.t_gwen_friday.lines.0": "In my kitchen with the telly and a whisky. I wasn’t up at the car park on Friday night. I don’t go up there in the dark.",
 "dialogues:topics.t_gwen_friday.prompt": "Where were you on Friday night?",
 "dialogues:topics.t_gwen_knock.lines.0": "No. Why would I? I’d said what I had to say in the note.",
 "dialogues:topics.t_gwen_knock.lines.1": "I keep thinking about it. If I’d knocked. If she’d come to the door and given me a mouthful, she’d have been awake. She’d have been alive.",
 "dialogues:topics.t_gwen_knock.prompt": "Did you knock on the van?",
 "dialogues:topics.t_gwen_vans.lines.0": "Forty of them on a summer night. Barbecues on my grass, rubbish over my wall, people doing their business behind my hedge. Last spring a loose dog had two of my ewes. I’ve asked the council, I’ve asked the parish, I’ve asked the police.",
 "dialogues:topics.t_gwen_vans.lines.1": "Nobody does anything. So I leave notes.",
 "dialogues:topics.t_gwen_vans.prompt": "Tell me about the vans at Marram Point.",
 "dialogues:topics.t_jo_co.lines.0": "It sticks to your blood better than oxygen does. First you get a headache. Then you feel sick and dizzy, and you put it down to anything — a cold, a hangover, a row. Then you get sleepy.",
 "dialogues:topics.t_jo_co.lines.1": "If you’re already asleep, you just don’t wake up. In a van that size, with a leak, it wouldn’t take long.",
 "dialogues:topics.t_jo_co.prompt": "What does carbon monoxide do to you?",
 "dialogues:topics.t_jo_friday.lines.0": "At home. All night. I’d been on nights all week and I was wrecked. I didn’t even know she’d gone to the coast until Saturday.",
 "dialogues:topics.t_jo_friday.prompt": "Where were you on Friday night?",
 "dialogues:topics.t_jo_tess.lines.0": "Three years. We had a flat in Barnstaple. We split up in September — her idea. She said I was too much. She moved into a house share with Megan.",
 "dialogues:topics.t_jo_tess.lines.1": "We were trying to be friends. I was trying.",
 "dialogues:topics.t_jo_tess.prompt": "Tell me about you and Tess.",
 "dialogues:topics.t_jo_thursday.lines.0": "Working. Night shift on the ambulance out of Barnstaple, seven till seven. Ask control — every job’s logged. Why Thursday?",
 "dialogues:topics.t_jo_thursday.prompt": "And on Thursday night?",
 "dialogues:topics.t_jo_visit.lines.0": "I said sorry. I said I’d change. She said she’d heard it all before. We both cried. It was boiling in there and it smelled of the heater — they all do, those diesel ones, Tess said. She’d had it on since before I got there.",
 "dialogues:topics.t_jo_visit.lines.1": "By the end we both had headaches. I thought it was the crying. I left at twenty to ten, and sat in my car, and nearly went back.",
 "dialogues:topics.t_jo_visit.prompt": "What happened in the van?",
 "dialogues:topics.t_jo_where.lines.0": "She’d posted a story from the car park. That’s all. It was right there for anyone to see.",
 "dialogues:topics.t_jo_where.prompt": "How did you know where she was?",
 "dialogues:topics.t_lou_business.lines.0": "Honestly? Dying. Everyone’s mortgage doubled this year, and a camper van’s the first thing people cancel. And now Trading Standards want every install record we’ve ever made.",
 "dialogues:topics.t_lou_business.lines.1": "Callum’s the best fitter in Devon. I’d stake my life on that heater. Somebody’s lying, or something’s very wrong.",
 "dialogues:topics.t_lou_business.lines.2": "And Kai — our apprentice — is off on his college block in Exeter this week. He hasn’t been in since Friday. The police never even spoke to him.",
 "dialogues:topics.t_lou_business.prompt": "How’s business?",
 "dialogues:topics.t_lou_doorbell.lines.0": "After the cameras went down, you mean? No. I went home and sat in the car on the drive till I’d stopped shaking, and then I went to bed.",
 "dialogues:topics.t_lou_doorbell.lines.1": "She opens the doorbell app on her phone and scrolls back to Thursday night, and holds it out to you.",
 "dialogues:topics.t_lou_doorbell.prompt": "Could you have come back to the yard later that night?",
 "dialogues:topics.t_lou_friday.lines.0": "Callum brought Moss round the front at half eight and she sat there all day, right in front of the workshop door. He was on the Jenkinses’ kitchen pod till Tess came. Nobody went near Moss — we’d have seen.",
 "dialogues:topics.t_lou_friday.prompt": "Tell me about Friday at the yard.",
 "dialogues:topics.t_lou_kai.lines.0": "I know. And I let him. Every time Callum said ‘Kai?’, I said nothing.",
 "dialogues:topics.t_lou_kai.lines.1": "I’ll tell Callum. I’ll tell Kai. I’ll pay back every penny. But I didn’t go anywhere near Moss.",
 "dialogues:topics.t_lou_kai.prompt": "Callum blamed Kai for the missing heaters.",
 "dialogues:topics.t_lou_policy.lines.0": "Every partnership has one. If one of us dies, the other can buy their share from the family, so the business doesn’t die too. That’s all it is.",
 "dialogues:topics.t_lou_policy.lines.1": "Two hundred thousand pounds, yes. I can do the sum as well as you can. I didn’t want Callum dead.",
 "dialogues:topics.t_lou_policy.prompt": "Tell me about the partnership insurance.",
 "dialogues:topics.t_lou_thursday.lines.0": "Home. All night. Sofa, telly, bed. Why?",
 "dialogues:topics.t_lou_thursday.prompt": "Where were you on Thursday night?",
 "dialogues:topics.t_lou_told.lines.0": "Me and Kai. Callum told us just after twelve — Tess had texted him. Pete had gone already; he went off to his mum’s just before twelve.",
 "dialogues:topics.t_lou_told.lines.1": "Callum didn’t tell anybody else, I don’t think. Why would he?",
 "dialogues:topics.t_lou_told.prompt": "Who knew Tess was taking Moss?",
 "dialogues:topics.t_pete_friday.lines.0": "Same as always. Opened up, kettle on, did my paperwork, couple of quick jobs, gone by twelve. Never went near Moss. Why would I?",
 "dialogues:topics.t_pete_friday.prompt": "Tell me about Friday morning.",
 "dialogues:topics.t_pete_kieran.lines.0": "His mum and me split up when he was three. I had him summers. Every August we came down here, and I’d watch him in the water and think, he’s braver than I ever was.",
 "dialogues:topics.t_pete_kieran.lines.1": "That year he wanted proper lessons, a proper school. I paid for the week. Rendle Surf.",
 "dialogues:topics.t_pete_kieran.lines.2": "Nobody ever answered for it. Callum Rendle went back to the beach like nothing had happened. That’s all I’m going to say.",
 "dialogues:topics.t_pete_kieran.prompt": "Tell me about Kieran.",
 "dialogues:topics.t_pete_mot.lines.0": "Second of November. She passed; one tyre on the way out, that’s all.",
 "dialogues:topics.t_pete_mot.lines.1": "An MOT doesn’t cover the living bits, mind — heaters, fridges, all that. Just the vehicle.",
 "dialogues:topics.t_pete_mot.prompt": "You did Moss’s MOT.",
 "dialogues:topics.t_pete_next_door.lines.0": "Year and a half. Best thing I ever did. Callum’s a good lad — helped me get my lift in, first week, and wouldn’t take a penny for it.",
 "dialogues:topics.t_pete_next_door.prompt": "How long have you been next door?",
 "dialogues:topics.t_pete_routine.lines.0": "Fridays? Dawn patrol. Regular as clockwork — boards on the roof by four, off to Marram Point, in the water at first light. Comes in on Saturdays smelling of wetsuit and grinning.",
 "dialogues:topics.t_pete_routine.prompt": "Did you know Callum’s habits?",
 "dialogues:topics.t_pete_rushed.lines.0": "That was me, and I’m sorry if it’s hurt him. He’s a lovely lad. But he’s been under pressure since the money went bad — working till ten at night — and his work’s been rushed. I’d told him so.",
 "dialogues:topics.t_pete_rushed.prompt": "Someone told the police Callum’s work was rushed. Was that you?",
 "dialogues:topics.t_pete_unit.lines.0": "Course. Help yourself — I’ve nothing to hide. I’m off to Barnstaple for a part anyway. Kettle’s on the counter.",
 "dialogues:topics.t_pete_unit.prompt": "Can I have a look round your garage?",
 "dialogues:topics.t_pete_weekend.lines.0": "Exeter. Mum’s in a care home there — dementia. I go every few weeks and stop over in a hotel. Left here at twelve on Friday, back on Sunday night. The home’ll tell you; so will the hotel.",
 "dialogues:topics.t_pete_weekend.prompt": "Where were you on Friday night?",
 "dialogues:topics.t_pete_why.lines.0": "Saw it on a website. I’d never set foot in North Devon before — sold up in Swindon, fancied the sea air, and the rent was half what I’d been paying. Simple as that.",
 "dialogues:topics.t_pete_why.prompt": "How did you come to take the unit next to Callum’s?",
 "evidence:evidence.abby_statement.detail": "Abby Rendle, Callum’s wife:\n\n‘Callum was home with me and Ruby on Friday night. He didn’t sleep — he never does when Tess is out in that van on her own; he kept checking the wind on his phone. Ruby and I used to go with him in the summer, before she started nursery.\n\n‘The business is in a bad way. Everyone’s mortgage went up this year, and nobody’s buying vans. Callum’s been blaming Kai for heaters going missing. I never believed that.\n\n‘And he doesn’t talk about the surf school. Not to me, not to anyone. Don’t ask me why.’",
 "evidence:evidence.abby_statement.name": "Abby’s account",
 "evidence:evidence.abby_statement.summary": "Callum’s wife, on Friday night, the business and the past.",
 "evidence:evidence.alarm_leaflet.detail": "‘Press the TEST button weekly. The test checks the electronics, the battery and the sounder. It does not expose the sensor to carbon monoxide. Never cover, paint or obstruct the sensor openings. An obstructed sensor will not detect carbon monoxide.’",
 "evidence:evidence.alarm_leaflet.name": "The alarm’s leaflet",
 "evidence:evidence.alarm_leaflet.summary": "What the TEST button does — and doesn’t — check.",
 "evidence:evidence.alarm_log.detail": "Unit 4 intruder alarm, Thursday 16 November:\n\n19:51 — SET (user: CALLUM)\n22:41 — UNSET (user: LOU)\n22:41–22:53 — movement: OFFICE CORRIDOR, STOCKROOM. Front door opened 22:44, 22:48, 22:51.\n22:49:10 — Rear door OPENED. 22:49:52 — Rear door CLOSED. 22:50 — movement: STOCKROOM.\nWorkshop: no movement.\n22:54 — SET (user: LOU)\nFriday 08:28 — UNSET (user: CALLUM)",
 "evidence:evidence.alarm_log.name": "Unit 4’s alarm log",
 "evidence:evidence.alarm_log.summary": "Thursday night: who unset the alarm, and which sensors fired.",
 "evidence:evidence.build_photos.detail": "Saltworks’ build album for Moss, October 2021 — the first van the business ever converted: two hundred photographs, one for every step. Photo 112 shows the heater’s exhaust joint with a new gasket in place, the clamp tightened, and a line of yellow paint across the nut and on to the bracket. Underneath is Callum’s checklist: ‘Gasket ✓ Clamp tightened ✓ Paint-marked ✓ Leak test ✓ CO alarm ✓’. Every install in every album has the same list.",
 "evidence:evidence.build_photos.name": "Callum’s build photos",
 "evidence:evidence.build_photos.summary": "Moss’s heater, photographed step by step on the day it was fitted.",
 "evidence:evidence.cafe_footage.detail": "The camera over the Lookout’s door covers the car park entrance and the end bays. Dan’s notes on what it shows — you check them against the clips on his laptop, and they’re accurate — Friday 17 to Saturday 18 November:\n\n16:03 — Moss arrives and parks in the end bay, nose to the sea.\n16:10–16:40 — a woman (Tess) walks along the cliff edge as the sun goes down, and back. No lights in Moss after that.\n19:30 — fairy lights on inside Moss. From about 19:35, the heater’s exhaust shows as a thin plume under the van.\n21:03 — a small white hatchback parks beside Moss. The driver, a woman carrying a bunch of flowers, gets into Moss.\n21:41 — she gets out without the flowers, sits in her car for a minute, and drives away.\n21:45 — Tess comes out, drops something in the bin, and goes back in.\n23:09 — a quad bike with one headlight stops beside Moss. A figure in a waxed jacket and a head torch goes to the windscreen. 23:11 — the quad rides off towards the field gate.\nNo other movement near Moss all night.\nSaturday 08:31 — Dan arrives to open up. 08:40 — Dan at Moss.",
 "evidence:evidence.cafe_footage.name": "The café’s camera",
 "evidence:evidence.cafe_footage.summary": "The car park from Friday afternoon to Saturday morning.",
 "evidence:evidence.callum_story.detail": "A story Callum posted at 17:41 on Thursday 16 November, filmed inside Moss in the rear yard. The heater roars; the camera holds on the CO alarm. The screen reads 0, and the slotted grille on its side is open and clear. Caption: ‘Weekly heater check ✅ Moss ready for dawn patrol 🌊’.",
 "evidence:evidence.callum_story.name": "Callum’s heater check",
 "evidence:evidence.callum_story.summary": "Thursday, 17:41: the alarm reads zero, its sensor clear.",
 "evidence:evidence.cartons.detail": "The recycling cage behind Unit 4, a few steps from Moss’s spot. On top of the cardboard: six flattened outer cartons for 2 kW diesel heaters, their shipping labels still on. The heaters left the yard in their own smaller boxes.",
 "evidence:evidence.cartons.name": "Cartons in the recycling cage",
 "evidence:evidence.cartons.summary": "Six empty heater cartons, flattened, behind Unit 4.",
 "evidence:evidence.co_alarm.detail": "A battery carbon monoxide alarm with a small screen, reading ‘0’. Press TEST and it shrieks. But the slotted grille on its left side, where the gas reaches the sensor, is covered by a strip of clear tape, cut neatly to the width of the grille. You only see it when the light catches it. On the back, a police sticker: ‘Function test — OK. 20/11.’",
 "evidence:evidence.co_alarm.name": "Moss’s CO alarm",
 "evidence:evidence.co_alarm.summary": "It works when you test it. But someone has covered its sensor.",
 "evidence:evidence.dan_dm.detail": "A direct message to Callum from Dan Kellow’s account, Wednesday 15 November, 23:14:\n\n‘Rendle Surf is back? Are you serious? After Kieran? You stood up at that inquest and put it all on me, and now you want to take other people’s kids out in the water again. You’ll get what’s coming to you, Callum. Mark my words.’\n\nCallum didn’t reply.",
 "evidence:evidence.dan_dm.name": "Dan’s message to Callum",
 "evidence:evidence.dan_dm.summary": "Wednesday night, after Callum announced the surf school.",
 "evidence:evidence.gasket.detail": "From the silt trap of the drain outside Pete’s back door: a thin round gasket the size of a large coin, with a hole in the middle and a ring of black soot on one face — fresh, not rusted. You photograph it where it lies and ring the coroner’s officer; by the afternoon it is in a police evidence bag. Shown the photograph, Callum goes white. ‘That’s a heater exhaust gasket. Twenty-four millimetre. The kind we fit.’",
 "evidence:evidence.gasket.name": "Gasket from the drain",
 "evidence:evidence.gasket.summary": "A small exhaust gasket, ringed with fresh soot.",
 "evidence:evidence.gate_log.detail": "Saltworks’ gate camera sees the gate and the fronts of Units 4 and 5. It can’t see the rear yard. Thursday 16 to Friday 17 November:\n\nThu 17:05 — Pete Stannard drives away in his car.\nThu 17:24 — Callum drives Moss round to the rear yard.\nThu 18:31 — Dan Kellow at the gate, on foot. Callum comes out, and they argue in the gateway. 18:37 — Callum turns his back and walks off round the side of Unit 4, towards the rear yard; Dan follows him, still shouting. Both out of shot. 18:40 — both back in view. 18:44 — Callum walks Dan to his car, and Dan drives off.\nThu 19:52 — Callum locks the gate and cycles away.\nThu 22:40 — Lou Bray’s car. She unlocks the gate and goes into Unit 4 by the front door. She carries boxes out to her car: two at 22:44, two at 22:48, two at 22:51. 22:55 — she locks up and drives off.\nFri 01:02 — CAMERA OFFLINE (no connection).\nFri 08:35 — camera back online: Callum has restarted the router. Moss is already parked in front of Unit 4.\nFri 11:58 — Pete drives away in his car.\nFri 15:31 — Tess arrives, dropped off. 15:44 — Tess drives Moss out of the gate.\nNobody else comes or goes while the camera is recording.",
 "evidence:evidence.gate_log.name": "The gate camera",
 "evidence:evidence.gate_log.summary": "Everyone who came through the yard gate, Thursday to Friday.",
 "evidence:evidence.gwen_sign.detail": "A board wired to the field gate, painted by hand in big block capitals: ‘WESTCOTT FARM. PRIVATE LAND. NO OVERNIGHT SLEEPING. NO FIRES. DOGS ON LEADS. THIS IS YOUR LAST WARNING.’ The same capitals and the same ‘LAST WARNING’ as the note the police found under Moss’s wiper.",
 "evidence:evidence.gwen_sign.name": "Sign on the field gate",
 "evidence:evidence.gwen_sign.summary": "Westcott Farm’s warning to vanlifers, in block capitals.",
 "evidence:evidence.heater_joint.detail": "Under Moss’s passenger seat, where the exhaust pipe meets the heater’s outlet, there is no gasket, and the clamp turns with your fingers. Someone once drew a line of yellow paint across the clamp nut and on to the bracket: the mark a fitter makes when a nut is tightened, so that anyone can see if it ever moves. The line is broken. The nut has been turned since it was painted, and its corners are bright where a spanner has bitten through the tarnish. Inside, the seat box is filmed with soot.",
 "evidence:evidence.heater_joint.name": "The heater’s exhaust joint",
 "evidence:evidence.heater_joint.summary": "No gasket, a loose clamp — and a broken paint mark.",
 "evidence:evidence.inquest_article.detail": "North Devon Journal, 15 October 2020. ‘Coroner criticises surf school after teenager drowns’.\n\n‘Kieran Moyle, 16, from Bristol, drowned in a rip current at Croyde on 21 August 2019 during a group lesson run by Rendle Surf. The inquest, held largely by video link, heard that a junior instructor, Daniel Kellow, 24, had been left alone with nine beginners while the school’s owner, Callum Rendle, taught a private lesson 300 metres away. Mr Rendle told the hearing the instructor had been “badly positioned”. The coroner recorded a conclusion of accidental death, but called the supervision “wholly inadequate”.\n\n‘Kieran’s mother, Sarah Moyle, said her son had been “the light of our lives”. His father, a mechanic from Swindon who asked not to be named, and who had brought him to Croyde every August since he was small, told the hearing by video link: “He was a strong swimmer. Somebody should answer for this.”’\n\nIn the margin, in Callum’s writing: ‘Every day.’",
 "evidence:evidence.inquest_article.name": "The newspaper cutting",
 "evidence:evidence.inquest_article.summary": "October 2020: the inquest into a drowning at Croyde.",
 "evidence:evidence.jo_flowers.detail": "A dozen white roses, still in the florist’s cellophane, on top of the rubbish in the car park bin. The card is still tucked inside: ‘I’m sorry. I’ll always be here. Jo x’",
 "evidence:evidence.jo_flowers.name": "Roses in the bin",
 "evidence:evidence.jo_flowers.summary": "A dozen white roses, thrown away unopened.",
 "evidence:evidence.kai_message.detail": "A message to Callum from Kai Huxtable, the Saltworks apprentice, Saturday 18 November, 14:02. Unread until now:\n\n‘Callum mate I’m so sorry about Tess 💔 is it true it was the heater?? Pete said Friday morning he’d had Moss’s seat base off for a look cos he thought he could smell coolant. He came in his back door with a torch and his knees all wet just as I got there, about 8. Did he tell u? Weird cos he always says ur installs are the tidiest on the estate. Anything u need just say’",
 "evidence:evidence.kai_message.name": "Kai’s message",
 "evidence:evidence.kai_message.summary": "Saturday afternoon, unread: what Kai saw on Friday morning.",
 "evidence:evidence.kieran_photo.detail": "A photo in a clip frame on the wall of Pete’s back office: a lanky teenage boy in a hired wetsuit, a foam surfboard under one arm, grinning, with Croyde Bay behind him. Written in marker along the bottom: ‘K — first lesson! Aug ’19’.",
 "evidence:evidence.kieran_photo.name": "Photo in Pete’s office",
 "evidence:evidence.kieran_photo.summary": "A teenage boy with a surfboard.",
 "evidence:evidence.kitchen_calendar.detail": "The Rendles’ calendar on the fridge. Every Friday since the first of October says ‘C — Marram 🌊’ in Callum’s capitals: his night in Moss at Marram Point, and the dawn surf after it. On Friday 17 November it’s crossed out, and underneath, in Abby’s writing: ‘Tess has Moss!’",
 "evidence:evidence.kitchen_calendar.name": "The family calendar",
 "evidence:evidence.kitchen_calendar.summary": "Every Friday since October: ‘C — Marram’.",
 "evidence:evidence.lou_doorbell.detail": "Lou’s front-door camera, Thursday 16 to Friday 17 November:\n\n23:14 — Lou’s car pulls on to the drive. She goes in.\nNo movement until 07:48 Friday, when her husband, Steve, takes the bins out.\n08:05 — Lou drives off.\nThe car never moves in between, and Barnstaple is six miles from the yard.",
 "evidence:evidence.lou_doorbell.name": "Lou’s doorbell camera",
 "evidence:evidence.lou_doorbell.summary": "Lou’s drive in Barnstaple, Thursday night to Friday morning.",
 "evidence:evidence.marketplace_ad.detail": "Callum finds it in two minutes on a selling site: ‘2 kW diesel night heater, top brand, brand new and boxed, £650 each (RRP £1,100), 6 available. Collection Braunton. No time-wasters.’ Seller: ‘L.B.’, a member since August 2023. In the background of one photo there’s the end of a stockroom shelf, and a Saltworks Vans sticker.",
 "evidence:evidence.marketplace_ad.name": "An online listing",
 "evidence:evidence.marketplace_ad.summary": "Brand-new heaters for sale, collection Braunton.",
 "evidence:evidence.megan_statement.detail": "Megan Hale, Tess’s housemate, a nurse at the same hospital:\n\n‘She came in at ten past eight on Friday morning after her last night and went straight to bed. When she woke up, about half past eleven, she came down and said, “I need the sea,” and texted Callum there and then. I dropped her at the yard at half past three. She’d have crashed out again the minute she lay down in that van.\n\n‘She and Callum had a row at their mum’s on Sunday, about money he owed her. It was her flat deposit. She was really upset.\n\n‘And Jo still had Tess on location sharing, from when they lived together. Tess kept meaning to turn it off. She said Jo still checked where she was, and it creeped her out.’",
 "evidence:evidence.megan_statement.name": "Megan’s account",
 "evidence:evidence.megan_statement.summary": "Tess’s housemate, on Tess’s last day and the people in her life.",
 "evidence:evidence.memorial_bench.detail": "A teak bench facing the sea, worn silver by the weather. The brass plaque reads: ‘KIERAN MOYLE · 2003–2019 · He loved these waves · Mum & Dad’. Fresh carnations are cable-tied to the arm, with a card in a plastic sleeve: ‘Every month. Still sorry. D.’",
 "evidence:evidence.memorial_bench.name": "The bench at the cliff edge",
 "evidence:evidence.memorial_bench.summary": "In memory of Kieran Moyle, 2003–2019.",
 "evidence:evidence.mot_certificate.detail": "MOT test certificate. VW Transporter, sage green. Test date: 2 November 2023. Result: PASS. Testing station: Stannard Motors, Unit 5, Braunton. Tester: P. Stannard. Advisory: ‘Nearside front tyre worn close to legal limit.’ Nothing else.",
 "evidence:evidence.mot_certificate.name": "Moss’s MOT certificate",
 "evidence:evidence.mot_certificate.summary": "Passed on 2 November, at the garage next door.",
 "evidence:evidence.partnership_policy.detail": "A ‘partner protection’ life policy taken out by Saltworks Vans in 2021. Lives insured: Callum Rendle and Louise Bray. On the death of either, £200,000 is paid to the surviving partner, to buy the dead partner’s share of the business from their family. Premiums paid up to date.",
 "evidence:evidence.partnership_policy.name": "Partnership insurance",
 "evidence:evidence.partnership_policy.summary": "If either partner dies, the other receives £200,000.",
 "evidence:evidence.police_summary.detail": "Devon and Cornwall Police — summary for the family of the deceased, given to Mrs R. Rendle by the coroner’s officer.\n\nSaturday 18 November 2023\n08:42 — 999 call from Daniel KELLOW (café owner): woman unresponsive in a camper van, Marram Point car park, Croyde.\n08:52 — Ambulance arrives. Deceased (Tess RENDLE, 31) pronounced dead at the scene. Diesel night heater still running. Carbon monoxide poisoning (carboxyhaemoglobin 64% on initial post-mortem blood test; full toxicology awaited). Estimated time of death 01:00–03:00.\nVehicle: VW Transporter camper ‘Moss’, owner Callum RENDLE (brother), converted by his firm, Saltworks Vans, Braunton. Borrowed by the deceased on Friday 17 November.\nUnder the windscreen wiper, a note in block capitals: ‘NO OVERNIGHT SLEEPING. LAST WARNING. NEXT TIME I WON’T BE SO POLITE.’ Author not identified; similar notes are often left on vehicles at that car park.\n\nMonday 20 November — vehicle examination at the police compound, Barnstaple: heater exhaust joint not sealed. No gasket present; clamp loose, with tool marks indicating recent adjustment. Exhaust gases escaping into the seat box and the living area. CO alarm present, batteries fitted, functional on test (test button); why it did not activate is unclear — possibly sited too far from the heater. Engine and cooling system: no faults found.\n\nTuesday 21 November — Mr C. RENDLE interviewed voluntarily under caution regarding the installation. Vehicle retained at the police compound pending the inquest.\n\nNo suspicion of deliberate harm. Enquiries continue into the installation. File passed to the coroner; Trading Standards informed regarding Saltworks Vans.",
 "evidence:evidence.police_summary.name": "Police summary",
 "evidence:evidence.police_summary.summary": "Devon and Cornwall Police’s account of Tess’s death, and their conclusion.",
 "evidence:evidence.stock_count.detail": "The stock sheet on the clipboard lists fourteen boxed 2 kW diesel heaters. There are eight on the shelf. Across the bottom of last week’s sheet, in Callum’s writing: ‘Heaters short AGAIN. Ask Kai.’",
 "evidence:evidence.stock_count.name": "The stockroom count",
 "evidence:evidence.stock_count.summary": "Fourteen heaters on the stock sheet. Eight on the shelf.",
 "evidence:evidence.surf_flyer.detail": "A box of glossy flyers, fresh from the printer: ‘RENDLE SURF IS BACK! Lessons for all ages from April 2024. Croyde’s original surf school.’ A photo of Callum grinning on a longboard. Taped to the lid is a printout of the post announcing it in September, with its two hundred and fourteen comments. One of them, from Dan Kellow, says only: ‘Unbelievable.’",
 "evidence:evidence.surf_flyer.name": "Rendle Surf flyer",
 "evidence:evidence.surf_flyer.summary": "‘Rendle Surf is back — spring 2024.’",
 "evidence:evidence.tape_roll.detail": "A roll of clear tape from the drawer under Pete’s workbench, narrower than parcel tape — about the width of the strip over the grille on Moss’s alarm. Its end has been cut square with a blade, not torn. You photograph it and leave it where it is.",
 "evidence:evidence.tape_roll.name": "Roll of clear tape",
 "evidence:evidence.tape_roll.summary": "From Pete’s bench drawer: narrow clear tape, cut square.",
 "evidence:evidence.tess_last_message.detail": "Tess to Megan, Friday 17 November, 22:10: ‘Jo turned up. With flowers. Unbelievable. Head’s pounding — going to sleep x’\n\nMegan replied at 22:30: ‘Omg!! Drink some water and ring me tomorrow 💚’ Her reply was never read.",
 "evidence:evidence.tess_last_message.name": "Tess’s last message",
 "evidence:evidence.tess_last_message.summary": "22:10 on Friday, to Megan.",
 "evidence:evidence.tess_messages.detail": "Messages between Tess and Callum on Friday 17 November. Ruth has Tess’s phone now.\n\nTess, 11:40: Finished my nights 🙌 Can I borrow Moss tonight? I need the sea.\nCallum, 11:52: Course! Heater’s fab 🔥 Keys at the yard from 3 x\nTess, 11:53: You’re the best. Megan’s dropping me 💚\nTess, 16:05: Here! She’s perfect. Thank you bro x\nCallum, 16:06: Enjoy. Sunrise is 07:41 🌊",
 "evidence:evidence.tess_messages.name": "Tess and Callum’s messages",
 "evidence:evidence.tess_messages.summary": "Friday: Tess asks to borrow Moss.",
 "evidence:evidence.tess_notebook.detail": "A notebook with a wave drawn on the cover. Most of it is shift notes and shopping lists. One page is headed ‘FLAT!!!’: ‘Offer accepted 💚 Completion 12 Jan. Deposit £18,000 — Callum by Christmas (he PROMISED).’ Further down, in a different pen, dated Sunday 12 November: ‘Lunch at Mum’s. Callum says he can’t. Not till spring, maybe. Told him what I think. Can’t do this again.’",
 "evidence:evidence.tess_notebook.name": "Tess’s notebook",
 "evidence:evidence.tess_notebook.summary": "The flat she was buying, and the money she was owed.",
 "evidence:evidence.tess_story.detail": "Tess’s story at 19:31 on Friday, screenshotted by Megan. Moss’s living area is strung with fairy lights; Tess, in a hoodie, holds up a mug and grins. Caption: ‘Tiny house by the sea 🌊 First night off in a fortnight. Heater on, kettle on 💚’. On the wall behind her shoulder, the CO alarm catches the fairy lights: a thin bright strip runs across its side. No location is tagged.",
 "evidence:evidence.tess_story.name": "Tess’s story",
 "evidence:evidence.tess_story.summary": "Friday, 19:31: ‘Heater on, kettle on.’",
 "suspects:suspects.callum.bio": "Tess’s older brother. He builds camper vans at Saltworks Vans, the business he runs with Lou Bray, and he converted Moss himself. The police say his work killed his sister. He has barely slept since Saturday.",
 "suspects:suspects.callum.dismissal": "I don’t know. I don’t know anything any more.",
 "suspects:suspects.callum.name": "Callum Rendle",
 "suspects:suspects.callum.rebuttal": "She died in my van, beside my heater. You think I don’t know that? I’d give anything to have been in there instead of her.",
 "suspects:suspects.callum.role": "The brother",
 "suspects:suspects.dan.bio": "He runs the Lookout, the café at Marram Point, and crews the inshore lifeboat at Appledore, where he lives. On Saturday morning he found Tess, pulled her out of the van and tried to bring her back. Years ago, he taught surfing for Callum.",
 "suspects:suspects.dan.dismissal": "Ask me something useful.",
 "suspects:suspects.dan.name": "Dan Kellow",
 "suspects:suspects.dan.rebuttal": "I pulled her out of that van with my own hands and tried to bring her back. Don’t you dare.",
 "suspects:suspects.dan.role": "The café owner",
 "suspects:suspects.gwen.bio": "Her family has farmed the land around Marram Point for four generations. She has fought the overnight vans for years — the litter, the fires, the dogs among her ewes — and the police found one of her notes on Moss’s windscreen.",
 "suspects:suspects.gwen.dismissal": "I’ve ewes to see to. Get to the point.",
 "suspects:suspects.gwen.name": "Gwen Westcott",
 "suspects:suspects.gwen.rebuttal": "I write notes. I’ve written hundreds. I never killed anybody, and I’d like to see you prove I did.",
 "suspects:suspects.gwen.role": "The farmer",
 "suspects:suspects.jo.bio": "A paramedic, and Tess’s girlfriend for three years, until they split up in September and Tess moved out into a house share. She still can’t talk about Tess in the past tense.",
 "suspects:suspects.jo.dismissal": "That’s got nothing to do with anything.",
 "suspects:suspects.jo.name": "Jo Tremlett",
 "suspects:suspects.jo.rebuttal": "I loved her. I’ve spent my whole working life keeping people alive. I would never have hurt her.",
 "suspects:suspects.jo.role": "The ex",
 "suspects:suspects.lou.bio": "Callum’s partner in Saltworks Vans: she does the carpentry and the books. With Trading Standards asking questions, she is trying to keep the business alive, and their apprentice, Kai, in a job.",
 "suspects:suspects.lou.dismissal": "I’ve got a business going under. Make it quick.",
 "suspects:suspects.lou.name": "Lou Bray",
 "suspects:suspects.lou.rebuttal": "I’d never hurt Callum, and I’d never have hurt Tess. Whatever you think I’ve done, it isn’t that.",
 "suspects:suspects.lou.role": "The business partner",
